Serguei Patchkovskii <patchkov@acs1.acs.ucalgary.ca> wrote:
> Philippe Adam (adam@club-internet.fr) wrote:
> : I have succeeded to boot with the SRM-console, and I have upgraded
> : to 5.3, but when I want to install DU 4.0E, I have a SCSI error on
> : the target 0 (my hard disk).

> : My SCSI card Qlogic 1040 is yet recognized (with a show conf and by
> : the kernel).

> 'show conf' showing a PCI card with the right ID alone is not a 
> guarantee it was properly recognized. If the card was successfully
> initialized, you should also see it listed in 'show dev' (as PKC-something,
> if my memory does not deceive me). 'show dev' should also show your
> hard drive as dkc0 (if uou set it to SCSI ID 0).

> If both show up all right, I would make sure that the SCSI chain is 
> properly terminated on *both* ends. In my experience, SRM is much
> less picky in this respect than the DU kernel. Check pkc_* variabled 
> in the SRM console (I *think* it should be pkc_term) - termination 
> should be set to 'on', and not to 'off' or 'diff'. While you are at
> it, make sure that other pkc variables are set to something reasonable.
> You should also have active termination at the end of the chain (either 
> on the last drive, if it supports active termination, or in the form of
> external terminator). 

> If you have a "real" au hanging around, you may also want to try to
> put your SCSI cable and drive in one and see whether it works or not.

> You may also want to examine isp1020_edit and ecu in your SRM console.
> I hadn't had to use them, but...

> Regards,

> /Serge.P

