Hi,

I have a heterogeneous network (solaris 2.5, solaris 7, linux, osf1 3.2
and SunOS) running off a Linux (userland nfsd, 2.0.36 kernel) fileserver.

All was well until I added a du4.0 client some time ago. At more or less
regular intervalls (~ once a day) the du4.0 client floods the network with
nfs traffic (I checked this with tcpdump once) going to the linux fileserver.

There were so many packets that the network (10MB ethernet) was  totally
flodded -- the backbone switch was lit up like a disco with all the
collisions! I have seen this effect with 4.0d and 4.0e -- the 4.0d
client did not last long and was running linux after one week of this
behaviour. Now I just got a couple of XP1000 boxes and the problem
reappeared after the first day! If anyone has any thoughts on this please
post and/or email me.

A final note: I have been running an osf3.2 box for a couple of years
w/o any hint of problem with nfs (same nfs server, same mounts). All
mounts are done using amd 6.0.1s5 (berkeley automounter) with options
"nfsv2,proto=udp". Please HELP!

